}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere some coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement “$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$” is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}=1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186244082 :question/problem "Express $z=10(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$" "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=10(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$"] [:db/cas 17592186244091 :question/problem "Express $z=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right))$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$" "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$"] [:db/cas 17592186244096 :question/problem "Express $S=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{5}{6}+⋯+\\frac{5}{26}+\\frac{5}{28}$ in sigma notation." "Express $$\n\\displaystyle S=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{5}{6}+⋯+\\frac{5}{26}+\\frac{5}{28}\n$$ in sigma notation."] [:db/cas 17592186244106 :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?"] [:db/cas 17592186244109 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$N=\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle N=\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244110 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244111 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244112 :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{det}(X)$, if\n    $X=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate $\\text{det}(X)$, if\n    $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244113 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =6$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" "Given that $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =6$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186244113 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-5)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{2}{9}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-5)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{2}{9}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For $\\lambda =6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244114 :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186244117 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}2x & {y}^{2} \\\\ z & -3\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}2x & {y}^{2} \\\\ z & -3\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244154 :question/problem "Calculate $det(T)$, if\n    $T=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 17 \\\\ 1 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate $det(T)$, if\n    $$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 17 \\\\ 1 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244228 :question/problem "Calculate the minor of the entry ${x}_{21}$ in the matrix\n$X=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 11\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate the minor of the entry ${x}_{21}$ in the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 11\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244229 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244233 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=(3xy-1)\\,i+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,j$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}.$" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(3xy-1)\\,i+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,j$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186244238 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x}-{y}^{2},{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the square with vertices at $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ $(1,1)$ and $(-1,1).$" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x}-{y}^{2},{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the square with vertices at $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ $(1,1)$ and $(-1,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186244243 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y={x}^{2}-x.$" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y={x}^{2}-x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186244244 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}2xy\\,dx+{y}^{2}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt{3x}.$" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}2xy\\,dx+{y}^{2}\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt{3x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186244245 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨2x+{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=3.$" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨2x+{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186244250 :question/problem "Calculate the minor of the entry ${b}_{22}$ in the matrix\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 13 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate the minor of the entry ${b}_{22}$ in the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 13 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244254 :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=y$ and $x=4y-{y}^{2}.$ Express ${∮}_{C}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dx+(2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=y$ and $x=4y-{y}^{2}.$ Express $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dx+(2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy\n$$ as a repeated integral."] [:db/cas 17592186244257 :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x+2.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}⟩,$ as a repeated integral." "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x+2.$ Express $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$  where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}⟩,$ as a repeated integral."] [:db/cas 17592186244258 :question/problem "Find $\\,|\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & x & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|$ given that $\\,|\\begin{aligned}x & -1 \\\\ 5 & y\\end{aligned}|=-3.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & x & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}x & -1 \\\\ 5 & y\\end{aligned}|=-3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244259 :question/problem "Find $\\,|\\begin{aligned}v & w & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}|$ given that $\\,|\\begin{aligned}v & w \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}|=3.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}v & w & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}|\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}v & w \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}|=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244260 :question/problem "Find $\\,|\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & y & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & z & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|$ given that $\\,|\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & y & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & z\\end{aligned}|=8.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & y & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & z & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & y & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & z\\end{aligned}|=8.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244261 :question/problem "Find $\\,|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & m & b & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & n & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|$ given that $\\,|\\begin{aligned}m & b \\\\ 0 & n\\end{aligned}|=-6.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & m & b & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & n & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}m & b \\\\ 0 & n\\end{aligned}|=-6.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244271 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244272 :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186244282 :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-(-3)I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-(-3)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-(-3)I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $6a-3b=6\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}-3\\cdot 2=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-(-3)I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-(-3)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-(-3)I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle 6a-3b=6\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}-3\\cdot 2=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247107 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$E=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle E=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247150 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $xyz=1$ since $0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x+y-z=0$ since $0+0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=-1$ since $0\\ne -1.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}B=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\x+y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $xyz=1$ since $0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x+y-z=0$ since $0+0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=-1$ since $0\\ne -1.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}B=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\x+y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186247172 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea5ff670f03d0bffb84f0929d19b94bca62f23677f913c01e8e0949d144bc60a.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cxy+\\frac{c{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(cx+\\frac{c}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(cx+\\frac{c}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{cx}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}+\\frac{c}{2} \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea5ff670f03d0bffb84f0929d19b94bca62f23677f913c01e8e0949d144bc60a.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cxy+\\frac{c{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(cx+\\frac{c}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(cx+\\frac{c}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{cx}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}+\\frac{c}{2} \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247173 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0\\}.$\n\n![](images/99/996bd092be07e2596e30739cc4249e8ed8ef859540067a94e0f9cdd05f9e1f84.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[-c{e}^{-x-y}]_{y=0}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =[-c{e}^{-x}]_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0\\}.$\n\n![](images/99/996bd092be07e2596e30739cc4249e8ed8ef859540067a94e0f9cdd05f9e1f84.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[-c{e}^{-x-y}]_{y=0}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =[-c{e}^{-x}]_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247174 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0\\}.$\n\n![](images/99/996bd092be07e2596e30739cc4249e8ed8ef859540067a94e0f9cdd05f9e1f84.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{[-\\frac{c}{2(1+x+y)^{2}}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{2(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{2(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{c}{2(1+x)}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0\\}.$\n\n![](images/99/996bd092be07e2596e30739cc4249e8ed8ef859540067a94e0f9cdd05f9e1f84.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{[-\\frac{c}{2(1+x+y)^{2}}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{2(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{2(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{c}{2(1+x)}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247175 :question/problem "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [1,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-y}{6},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$" "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [1,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-y}{6},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247175 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{xy}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $g(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[y]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\nh(0,2)=\\frac{0-2}{6}=-\\frac{1}{3}≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\"" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{xy}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $g(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[y]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\nh(0,2)=\\frac{0-2}{6}=-\\frac{1}{3}≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186247176 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[3x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[2{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\ng(0,1)=0-1=-1≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $h(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot y]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[3x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[2{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\ng(0,1)=0-1=-1≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $h(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot y]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186247177 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.$\n\n![](images/a4/a49e679b3919594edc7fe963190a754324b9b5ea980d1936121c040d0ee52284.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nW\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[-c\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-c\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-c\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[c\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{c\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =c\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )}{2} \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.$\n\n![](images/a4/a49e679b3919594edc7fe963190a754324b9b5ea980d1936121c040d0ee52284.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nW\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[-c\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-c\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-c\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[c\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{c\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =c\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )}{2} \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247189 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x<2y\\le 2\\}.$\n\n![](images/60/60acb4126a456e51e00db8bbeb2f8dbc6a773a4792dda7838d2aa6a3553fa739.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,dx\\,dy & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}[{\\int }_{0}^{2y}{e}^{x}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}[{e}^{x}]_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}({e}^{2y}-{e}^{0})dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-{e}^{-y})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-{e}^{-y})\\,dy & =c[{e}^{y}+{e}^{-y}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =c({e}^{1}+{e}^{-1})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =c(e+{e}^{-1}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc(e+{e}^{-1}-2)=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}-2}.\n$$" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x<2y\\le 2\\}.$\n\n![](images/60/60acb4126a456e51e00db8bbeb2f8dbc6a773a4792dda7838d2aa6a3553fa739.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,dx\\,dy & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}[{\\int }_{0}^{2y}{e}^{x}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}[{e}^{x}]_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}({e}^{2y}-{e}^{0})dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-{e}^{-y})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-{e}^{-y})\\,dy & =c[{e}^{y}+{e}^{-y}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =c({e}^{1}+{e}^{-1})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =c(e+{e}^{-1}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc(e+{e}^{-1}-2)=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}-2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247193 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le y\\}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1ef0c7b289e24a98366c64b9a213c4f67d2fc63157b3319f0118eb16c514097e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}c(1-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}c(1-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cx-\\frac{cy{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x={y}^{2}}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-({y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2}))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy & =c{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{6}}{12}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}-\\frac{c}{8}-\\frac{c}{3}+\\frac{c}{12}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{8}=1\\,⇒\\,c=8.\n$$" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le y\\}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1ef0c7b289e24a98366c64b9a213c4f67d2fc63157b3319f0118eb16c514097e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}c(1-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}c(1-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cx-\\frac{cy{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x={y}^{2}}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-({y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2}))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy & =c{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{6}}{12}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}-\\frac{c}{8}-\\frac{c}{3}+\\frac{c}{12}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{8}=1\\,⇒\\,c=8.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247194 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}\\}.$\n\n![](images/26/268e469b0306d22e6b6dc05905a9d56a1cabd4b0e405f741ff458ca241440d4a.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx=1.\n$$\n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)]_{y=e}^{y={e}^{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx & ={[\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡e}{2}-\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}\\}.$\n\n![](images/26/268e469b0306d22e6b6dc05905a9d56a1cabd4b0e405f741ff458ca241440d4a.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx=1.\n$$\n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)]_{y=e}^{y={e}^{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx & ={[\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡e}{2}-\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247195 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\}.$\n\n![](images/8a/8a94024cbce0389b1d9c60bf64a38cb7c8e8e52836b2b39e9e638f856a1c5269.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}cx{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}cx{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{e}^{y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx[{e}^{y}]_{y=0}^{y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-{e}^{0})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx(x-1)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(c{x}^{2}-cx)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(c{x}^{2}-cx)dx & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8c}{3}-\\frac{4c}{2})-(\\frac{c}{3}-\\frac{c}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5c}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5c}{6}=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\}.$\n\n![](images/8a/8a94024cbce0389b1d9c60bf64a38cb7c8e8e52836b2b39e9e638f856a1c5269.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}cx{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}cx{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{e}^{y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx[{e}^{y}]_{y=0}^{y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-{e}^{0})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx(x-1)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(c{x}^{2}-cx)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(c{x}^{2}-cx)dx & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8c}{3}-\\frac{4c}{2})-(\\frac{c}{3}-\\frac{c}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5c}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5c}{6}=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247244 :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$, \nfind a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$" "Given the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nfind a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186247267 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247268 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247269 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247270 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247271 :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247274 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247275 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247326 :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{3{x}^{4}-x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-0}{3-0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{3{x}^{4}-x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\n$$ and $\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-0}{3-0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186247327 :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{7}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{7},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7x+3}{6{x}^{7}-2{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7x}{{x}^{7}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{7}}{{x}^{7}}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{7}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(6-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}},\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(6-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{6-0-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{7}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{7},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7x+3}{6{x}^{7}-2{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7x}{{x}^{7}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{7}}{{x}^{7}}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{7}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(6-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{{x}^{6}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}},\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}\n$$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(6-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{6-0-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186247354 :question/problem "$\\int 48(3x-2)^{7}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 48(3x-2)^{7}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247355 :question/problem "$\\int 6(5-3x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 6(5-3x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247357 :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4}{kx-3}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{5})\\cup (\\frac{3}{5},\\infty ).$ What is the value of $k?$" "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4}{kx-3}$ is $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{5})\\cup (\\frac{3}{5},\\infty ).\n$$ What is the value of $k?$"] [:db/cas 17592186247358 :question/problem "$\\int 9(3-2x)^{8}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 9(3-2x)^{8}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247360 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{1}{(5x-1)^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{(5x-1)^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247361 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+3)^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{(2x+3)^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247365 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant."] [:db/cas 17592186247366 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant."] [:db/cas 17592186247367 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4}{5x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4}{5x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant."] [:db/cas 17592186247368 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{1-4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3}{1-4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant."] [:db/cas 17592186247369 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{3x+2}+\\frac{1}{3x-2})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\frac{1}{3x+2}+\\frac{1}{3x-2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant."] [:db/cas 17592186247370 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{x+3}+\\frac{1}{x-3})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\frac{1}{x+3}+\\frac{1}{x-3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant."] [:db/cas 17592186247374 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247374 :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+7$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+7}+\\frac{C}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+7)(x+1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+17 & =(Ax+B)(x+1)+C({x}^{2}+7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-1,B=3,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)}=\\frac{-x+3}{{x}^{2}+7}+\\frac{2}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{-x+3}{{x}^{2}+7}+\\frac{2}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use a substitution $u={x}^{2}+7.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+7| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+7)+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}\\right)+2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+7)+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{7}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}\\right)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+7$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+7}+\\frac{C}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+7)(x+1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+17 & =(Ax+B)(x+1)+C({x}^{2}+7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-1,B=3,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)}=\\frac{-x+3}{{x}^{2}+7}+\\frac{2}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{-x+3}{{x}^{2}+7}+\\frac{2}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use a substitution $u={x}^{2}+7.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+7| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+7)+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}\\right)+2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+7)+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{7}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}\\right)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247375 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 12{x}^{5}\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 12{x}^{5}\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247376 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+42}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+42}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247376 :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+9$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+42}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-3)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+9}+\\frac{C}{x-3}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+9)(x-3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x+42=(Ax+B)(x-3)+C({x}^{2}+9).\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-2,B=-5,$ and $C=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+42}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-3)}=-\\frac{2x+5}{{x}^{2}+9}+\\frac{3}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+42}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{2x+5}{{x}^{2}+9}+\\frac{3}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+9}\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use a substitution $u={x}^{2}+9.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+9| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+42}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+9}\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+9)-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+3\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+9)-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+9$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+42}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-3)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+9}+\\frac{C}{x-3}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+9)(x-3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x+42=(Ax+B)(x-3)+C({x}^{2}+9).\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-2,B=-5,$ and $C=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+42}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-3)}=-\\frac{2x+5}{{x}^{2}+9}+\\frac{3}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+42}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{2x+5}{{x}^{2}+9}+\\frac{3}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+9}\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use a substitution $u={x}^{2}+9.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+9| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+42}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+9}\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+9)-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+3\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+9)-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247394 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{-9x+27}{(1-x)^{2}(x+5)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{-9x+27}{(1-x)^{2}(x+5)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247396 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 2{x}^{5}(4+{x}^{6})^{5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2{x}^{5}(4+{x}^{6})^{5}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247397 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {x}^{6}(2+{x}^{7})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{6}(2+{x}^{7})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247398 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+10}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+10}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247399 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1-4{x}^{3}}{1+x-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1-4{x}^{3}}{1+x-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247400 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}-1}{{x}^{5}-x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}-1}{{x}^{5}-x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247401 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{2x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247402 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{7{x}^{4}}{{(3-{x}^{5})}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{7{x}^{4}}{{(3-{x}^{5})}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247403 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247404 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{14{x}^{2}+47x+42}{(2x+3)^{2}(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{14{x}^{2}+47x+42}{(2x+3)^{2}(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247405 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}y.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247405 :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$" "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247406 :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{3{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{3{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186247408 :question/problem "Given that $u=x+1,$ which integral is equivalent to $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Given that $u=x+1,$ which integral is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250118 :question/problem "What is $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}z}÷\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{6{z}^{3}}?$" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}z}÷\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{6{z}^{3}}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250122 :question/problem "