n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250187 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250189 :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=3,$ then what is ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=3,\n$$ then what is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250190 :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8$ and ${\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4,$ then what is ${\\int }_{3}^{6}(5-2f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4,\n$$ then what is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{6}(5-2f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250191 :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1,$ then what is ${\\int }_{-1}^{3}(2f(x)-3)\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1,\n$$ then what is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{3}(2f(x)-3)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250192 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{8}^{5}3f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{3}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{8}^{5}3f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3.\n$$ What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250193 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{4}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$ and ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6.\n$$ What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250194 :question/problem "$\\int (x+\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (x+\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250195 :question/problem "$\\int -\\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int -\\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250196 :question/problem "$\\int -\\frac{12}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int -\\frac{12}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250197 :question/problem "$\\int (3{x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}})\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (3{x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}})\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250198 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250199 :question/problem "${\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{t}{8}\\,\\text{d}t=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{t}{8}\\,\\text{d}t=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250200 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250201 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250202 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250203 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250204 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-2}^{1}(6{z}^{2}-5{z}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{1}(6{z}^{2}-5{z}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250205 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{8}-3)\\,\\text{d}t=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{8}-3)\\,\\text{d}t=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250206 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(2-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(2-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250223 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 6x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 6x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250223 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 6x{e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe, substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 6x\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{-3x}+2\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-2x{e}^{-3x}+2\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3x}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 6x{e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe, substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 6x\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{-3x}+2\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-2x{e}^{-3x}+2\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3x}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250224 :question/problem "$\\int 4x{e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 4x{e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250224 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 4x{e}^{8x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=4 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{8x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 4x\\cdot {e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{e}^{8x}-\\int \\frac{1}{8}{e}^{8x}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x{e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{8x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{8x}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{8x}}{16}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 4x{e}^{8x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=4 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{8x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 4x\\cdot {e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{e}^{8x}-\\int \\frac{1}{8}{e}^{8x}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x{e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{8x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{8x}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{8x}}{16}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250225 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250225 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x{3}^{x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′} & ={3}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\int \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\int \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{3}^{x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′} & ={3}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\int \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\int \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250226 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int (x-2){e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (x-2){e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250226 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int (x-2){e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x-2\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{x+3}\\, & ⟹\\,v={e}^{x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (x-2)\\cdot {e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =(x-2)\\cdot {e}^{x+3}-\\int {e}^{x+3}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x-2){e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =(x-2){e}^{x+3}-\\int {e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x-2){e}^{x+3}-{e}^{x+3}+C \\\\  & =(x-3){e}^{x+3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (x-2){e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x-2\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{x+3}\\, & ⟹\\,v={e}^{x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (x-2)\\cdot {e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =(x-2)\\cdot {e}^{x+3}-\\int {e}^{x+3}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x-2){e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =(x-2){e}^{x+3}-\\int {e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x-2){e}^{x+3}-{e}^{x+3}+C \\\\  & =(x-3){e}^{x+3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250227 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (3x-4){e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (3x-4){e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250227 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int (3x-4){e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x-4\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (3x-4)\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =(3x-4)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3x-4){e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{(3x-4){e}^{-3x}}{3}+\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{(3x-4){e}^{-3x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{(3x-3){e}^{-3x}}{3}+C \\\\  & =(1-x){e}^{-3x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (3x-4){e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x-4\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (3x-4)\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =(3x-4)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3x-4){e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{(3x-4){e}^{-3x}}{3}+\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{(3x-4){e}^{-3x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{(3x-3){e}^{-3x}}{3}+C \\\\  & =(1-x){e}^{-3x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250228 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250228 :question/worked-solution "We use the identities $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{3}⁡x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identities $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{3}⁡x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250229 :question/problem "$\\int 3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250229 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250230 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250230 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\int \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\int \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250231 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250231 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250232 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250232 :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =3[\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡0] \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =3[\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡0] \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250233 :question/problem "$\\int (x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250233 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int (x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x+4\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (x+4)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(x+4)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x+4\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (x+4)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(x+4)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250234 :question/problem "$\\int (2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250234 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int (2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x+3\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (2x+3)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(2x+3)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =-(2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x+3\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (2x+3)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(2x+3)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =-(2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250235 :question/problem "$\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250235 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{25}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{25}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250236 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250236 :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250237 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250237 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x{csc}^{2}⁡x\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={csc}^{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ where $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{csc}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{csc}^{2}⁡x\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={csc}^{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ where $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{csc}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250238 :question/problem "${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250238 :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-[\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)] \\\\  & =-[1-\\sqrt{2}] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\n$$ to express this as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-[\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)] \\\\  & =-[1-\\sqrt{2}] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250239 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-1}^{0}3x{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{0}3x{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250240 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250242 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250243 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/3}9x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}9x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250244 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250251 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250252 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250264 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250265 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-{sec}^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-{sec}^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250266 :question/problem "$\\int (1+2x+{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (1+2x+{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250267 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250268 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cos}^{3}⁡x+{tan}^{2}⁡x{cos}^{3}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cos}^{3}⁡x+{tan}^{2}⁡x{cos}^{3}⁡x)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250269 :question/problem "${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cot}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cot}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250270 :question/problem "${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2+3{x}^{2}+2{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2+3{x}^{2}+2{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250271 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{cot}^{5}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{(1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{cot}^{5}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{(1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250272 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}({sin}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x{sin}^{3}⁡x)\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}({sin}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x{sin}^{3}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250273 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡(3x+2)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x+2){sin}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡(3x+2)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x+2){sin}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250274 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250275 :question/problem "$\\int (8+8{cot}^{2}⁡(4x+3))\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (8+8{cot}^{2}⁡(4x+3))\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250277 :question/problem "Determine $\\int \\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡(5x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-2)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Determine $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡(5x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-2)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250281 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250282 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 2x{log}_{5}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2x{log}_{5}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250283 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x{log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250284 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 8{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 8{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250285 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{log}_{4}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{log}_{4}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250286 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250287 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250288 :question/problem "$\\int {log}_{3}⁡(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {log}_{3}⁡(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250289 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250290 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1/3}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/3}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250291 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250294 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250295 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250297 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{2}\\right)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{2}\\right)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250298 :question/problem "$\\int (3{x}^{2}-{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (3{x}^{2}-{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250299 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (4-{cos}^{2}⁡4x+{sin}^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4-{cos}^{2}⁡4x+{sin}^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250300 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({sin}^{2}⁡4x-{cos}^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({sin}^{2}⁡4x-{cos}^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250301 :question/problem "$\\int 4{cos}^{2}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 4{cos}^{2}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250302 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int (4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250302 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+1)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}\\right)(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}{2}-4\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}\\right)(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{2x}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{2x}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}(2x-1)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}{2}-4(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}(2x-1)+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}{2}-{e}^{2x}(2x-1)-4D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(4{x}^{2}-4x+3)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-4D.$" "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+1)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}\\right)(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}{2}-4\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}\\right)(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{2x}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{2x}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}(2x-1)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}{2}-4(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}(2x-1)+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}{2}-{e}^{2x}(2x-1)-4D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(4{x}^{2}-4x+3)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-4D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186250303 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int ({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250303 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}-4\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4)({e}^{x})-\\int ({e}^{x})(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}-2\\int x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x({e}^{x})-\\int ({e}^{x})(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{e}^{x}-\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{e}^{x}-{e}^{x}+D \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(x-1)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}-2({e}^{x}(x-1)+D) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}-2(x-1){e}^{x}-2D \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x-2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$" "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}-4\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4)({e}^{x})-\\int ({e}^{x})(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}-2\\int x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x({e}^{x})-\\int ({e}^{x})(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{e}^{x}-\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{e}^{x}-{e}^{x}+D \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(x-1)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}-2({e}^{x}(x-1)+D) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}-2(x-1){e}^{x}-2D \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x-2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186250304 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 4{cos}^{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 4{cos}^{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250305 :question/problem "$\\int {cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250306 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int (1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250306 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=1-2{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(1-2{x}^{2})\\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right)(-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}}{4}+\\int x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right)(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{4x}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{4x}}{4}-\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(4x-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}}{4}+(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(4x-1)+C) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}}{4}+\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(4x-1)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(-8{x}^{2}+4x+3)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=1-2{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(1-2{x}^{2})\\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right)(-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}}{4}+\\int x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right)(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{4x}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{4x}}{4}-\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(4x-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}}{4}+(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(4x-1)+C) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}}{4}+\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(4x-1)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(-8{x}^{2}+4x+3)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250307 :question/problem "$\\int ({sin}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({sin}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250308 :question/problem "$\\int (4{sin}^{2}⁡x+6x)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4{sin}^{2}⁡x+6x)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250309 :question/problem "$\\int 2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250310 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)-1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)-1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250311 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x+1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x+1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250313 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250313 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int {log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{4}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{4}⁡(x))(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(x{log}_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D) \\\\  & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2x{log}_{4}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{2x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}-\\frac{2D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =x({({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡4})+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$" "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int {log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{4}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{4}⁡(x))(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(x{log}_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D) \\\\  & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2x{log}_{4}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{2x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}-\\frac{2D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =x({({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡4})+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186250314 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250314 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}))(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}))(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3x+D) \\\\  & =x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})+18x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-6D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})+18x]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[e{ln}^{2}⁡({e}^{3})-6e\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3})+18e]-[(1){ln}^{2}⁡({1}^{3})-6(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{3})+18(1)] \\\\  & =9e-18e+18e-18 \\\\  & =9e-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}))(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}))(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3x+D) \\\\  & =x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})+18x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-6D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})+18x]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[e{ln}^{2}⁡({e}^{3})-6e\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3})+18e]-[(1){ln}^{2}⁡({1}^{3})-6(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{3})+18(1)] \\\\  & =9e-18e+18e-18 \\\\  & =9e-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250315 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3x-4}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3x-4}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250316 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2x+5}{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2x+5}{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250317 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{6x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{6x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250318 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\frac{8x+1}{4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\frac{8x+1}{4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250319 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250319 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x=2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{3}⁡x)}^{2}(2{x}^{2})-\\int (2{x}^{2})\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{log}_{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{3}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{4D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$ \n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1)-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2-2+1)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(0-0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x=2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{3}⁡x)}^{2}(2{x}^{2})-\\int (2{x}^{2})\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{log}_{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{3}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{4D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$ \n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1)-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2-2+1)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(0-0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250320 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+18}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+18}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250321 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{(6x+1)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{(6x+1)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250322 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{|x-3|\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2}{|x-3|\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250323 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250324 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{|x-4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{|x-4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250325 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{|x+2|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{|x+2|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250326 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250326 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=9{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 9{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=3{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}(3{x}^{3})-\\int (3{x}^{3})\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{2}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{x}^{3}{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D) \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{6D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{3})]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{2}{3})-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3-2+\\frac{2}{3})-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(0-0+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}-2}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=9{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 9{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=3{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}(3{x}^{3})-\\int (3{x}^{3})\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{2}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{x}^{3}{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D) \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{6D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{3})]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{2}{3})-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3-2+\\frac{2}{3})-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(0-0+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}-2}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250327 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250327 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{4{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int {log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})-\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})-\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =x{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{4x{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{8x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{8x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}({ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+8)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{4D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}({ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+8)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}({ln}^{2}⁡({e}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})+8)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}({ln}^{2}⁡({1}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2})+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(4-8+8)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(0-0+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{4e-8}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{4{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int {log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})-\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})-\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =x{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{4x{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{8x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{8x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}({ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+8)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{4D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}({ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+8)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}({ln}^{2}⁡({e}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})+8)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}({ln}^{2}⁡({1}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2})+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(4-8+8)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(0-0+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{4e-8}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250328 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250328 :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=25{x}^{4} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int 25{x}^{4}\\text{d}x=5{x}^{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(5{x}^{5})-\\int (5{x}^{5})\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-10\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{4} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{4}\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {x}^{4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{25}+D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-10\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-10(\\frac{{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{25}+D) \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{5}-10D \\\\  & ={x}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{5})+C\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-10D.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[{x}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{5})]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[{e}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{2}{5})]-[{1}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{2}{5})] \\\\  & =[{e}^{5}(5-2+\\frac{2}{5})]-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{17{e}^{5}-2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=25{x}^{4} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int 25{x}^{4}\\text{d}x=5{x}^{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(5{x}^{5})-\\int (5{x}^{5})\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-10\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{4} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{4}\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {x}^{4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{25}+D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-10\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-10(\\frac{{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{25}+D) \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{5}-10D \\\\  & ={x}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{5})+C\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-10D.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[{x}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{5})]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[{e}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{2}{5})]-[{1}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{2}{5})] \\\\  & =[{e}^{5}(5-2+\\frac{2}{5})]-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{17{e}^{5}-2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250329 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250330 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ using integration by parts."] [:db/cas 17592186250331 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ using integration by parts."] [:db/cas 17592186250331 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{6}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6{ln}^{5}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{6}⁡x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{6{ln}^{5}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{7}⁡x-6\\int \\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{7}⁡x-6I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{7}⁡x-6I \\\\ 7I & ={ln}^{7}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{7}⁡x}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{7}({ln}^{7}⁡x){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}({ln}^{7}⁡(e)-{ln}^{7}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{6}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6{ln}^{5}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{6}⁡x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{6{ln}^{5}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{7}⁡x-6\\int \\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{7}⁡x-6I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{7}⁡x-6I \\\\ 7I & ={ln}^{7}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{7}⁡x}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{7}({ln}^{7}⁡x){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}({ln}^{7}⁡(e)-{ln}^{7}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250332 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ using integration by parts."] [:db/cas 17592186250332 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{9}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{9{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{9}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{9{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4}-9\\int \\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4}-9I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4}-9I \\\\ 10I & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{40}({ln}^{10}⁡x){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}({ln}^{10}⁡(e)-{ln}^{10}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{9}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{9{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{9}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{9{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4}-9\\int \\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4}-9I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4}-9I \\\\ 10I & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{40}({ln}^{10}⁡x){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}({ln}^{10}⁡(e)-{ln}^{10}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250351 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1?$" "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250353 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$ is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- Integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$ is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}$ is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}$ only." "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\n$$ is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- Integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\n$$ is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\n$$ is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186250359 :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x+1}\\le \\frac{1}{9}.$" "Solve the inequality $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x+1}\\le \\frac{1}{9}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250371 :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." "Simplify the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ using symmetry."] [:db/cas 17592186250372 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250373 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250374 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250375 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)}{1+(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ is an odd function, but integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x$ is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$; ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ only." "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)}{1+(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ is an odd function, but integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is not of the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186250379 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={sin}^{5}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{3}+(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+(-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(-x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x$; ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x$ only." "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={sin}^{5}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is not of the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{3}+(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+(-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(-x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186250380 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & ={e}^{-x}-{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}-{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$; ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x$ only." "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & ={e}^{-x}-{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}-{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{3}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186250381 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\sqrt[3]{-x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, but integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$ is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ only." "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\sqrt[3]{-x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, but integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is not of the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186250389 :question/problem "What is the center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{16}=1?$" "What is the center of the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{16}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250390 :question/problem "What is the center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1?$" "What is the center of the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250391 :question/problem "What is the center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{36}=1?$" "What is the center of the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{36}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250393 :question/problem "What is the center of the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{25}=1?$" "What is the center of the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{25}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250394 :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{3}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{3}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes."] [:db/cas 17592186250397 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4x-10}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4x-10}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250399 :question/problem "${\\int }_{2/3}^{2}\\frac{3}{(3x-2)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2/3}^{2}\\frac{3}{(3x-2)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250424 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250425 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250425 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)]^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\right)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\infty \n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\infty ,\n$$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)]^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\right)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250427 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250428 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250431 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250438 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250454 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250455 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250456 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{x-2}{(x+8)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{x-2}{(x+8)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250457 :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}=\\frac{2x+B}{{x}^{2}+5}-\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}=\\frac{2x+B}{{x}^{2}+5}-\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a constant to be found, calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250457 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $B.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x-2 & =(2x+B)(x+1)-({x}^{2}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+5(0)-2 & =(2(0)+B)(0+1)-({0}^{2}+5) \\\\ -2 & =B-5 \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x+3}{{x}^{2}+5}-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+5=u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the constant $B.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x-2 & =(2x+B)(x+1)-({x}^{2}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+5(0)-2 & =(2(0)+B)(0+1)-({0}^{2}+5) \\\\ -2 & =B-5 \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x+3}{{x}^{2}+5}-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+5=u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250458 :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{D}{x+2},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{D}{x+2},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant to be found, calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250458 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $D.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+20 & =(x-2)(x+2)+D({x}^{2}+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}+20 & =(0-2)(0+2)+D({0}^{2}+8) \\\\ 20 & =-4+8D \\\\ 24 & =8D \\\\ D & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{3}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+8=u.$ Then, $x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\text{d}x=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4}\\right)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the constant $D.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+20 & =(x-2)(x+2)+D({x}^{2}+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}+20 & =(0-2)(0+2)+D({0}^{2}+8) \\\\ 20 & =-4+8D \\\\ 24 & =8D \\\\ D & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{3}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+8=u.$ Then, $x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\text{d}x=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4}\\right)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250460 :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}=\\frac{2x+B}{{x}^{2}+20}-\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}=\\frac{2x+B}{{x}^{2}+20}-\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a constant to be found, calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250460 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $B.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x-27 & =(2x+B)(x+1)-({x}^{2}+20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-5(0)-27 & =(2(0)+B)(0+1)-({0}^{2}+20) \\\\ -27 & =B-20 \\\\ B & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-7}{{x}^{2}+20}-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x-7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+20=u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the constant $B.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x-27 & =(2x+B)(x+1)-({x}^{2}+20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-5(0)-27 & =(2(0)+B)(0+1)-({0}^{2}+20) \\\\ -27 & =B-20 \\\\ B & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-7}{{x}^{2}+20}-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x-7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+20=u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250465 :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+1$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{C}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+1)(x-4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-9x+5 & =(Ax+B)(x-4)+C({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=-1,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)} & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+1| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+1$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{C}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+1)(x-4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-9x+5 & =(Ax+B)(x-4)+C({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=-1,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)} & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+1| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250466 :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+6$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+6}+\\frac{C}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+6)(x-3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-7x & =(Ax+B)(x-3)+C({x}^{2}+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=-4,$ and $C=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)} & =\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}+6}-\\frac{2}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}+6}-\\frac{2}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+6.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+6| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+6$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+6}+\\frac{C}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+6)(x-3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-7x & =(Ax+B)(x-3)+C({x}^{2}+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=-4,$ and $C=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)} & =\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}+6}-\\frac{2}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}+6}-\\frac{2}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+6.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+6| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250467 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250467 :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+3$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{x+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+3)(x+4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+5x-9 & =(Ax+B)(x+4)+C({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=-3,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)} & =\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+3| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+3$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{x+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+3)(x+4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+5x-9 & =(Ax+B)(x+4)+C({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=-3,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)} & =\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+3| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250469 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral  $\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250470 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral  $\\int \\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250481 :question/problem "Solve for $m$ where $\\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}}=\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}}.$" "Solve for $m$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}}=\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250484 :question/problem "Which value of $z$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}?$" "Which value of $z$ satisfies the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250509 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {\\left(\\frac{D}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{6}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{6}{5\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {\\left(\\frac{D}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{6}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{6}{5\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250521 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{6}=1?$" "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{6}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250522 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(y-8)^{2}}{4}=1?$" "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(y-8)^{2}}{4}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250523 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{3}=1?$" "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{3}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250524 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}=1?$" "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250525 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}=1?$" "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250526 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{36}=1?$" "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{36}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250527 :question/problem "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}?$" "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250528 :question/problem "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}?$" "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250542 :question/problem "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{35}{2}\\ge \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x.$" "Find the solution of $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{35}{2}\\ge \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186250542 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{35}{2} & \\ge \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x-\\frac{35}{2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+2x-35) & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x+7)(x-5) & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+7)(x-5) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+7)(x-5).$ The roots are $x=-7$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-7),$ $(-7,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+7)(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+7)(x-5)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+7)(x-5)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-7)$ and $(5,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-7$ and $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{35}{2}\\ge \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [5,\\infty ).$" "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{35}{2} & \\ge \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x-\\frac{35}{2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+2x-35) & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x+7)(x-5) & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+7)(x-5) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+7)(x-5).$ The roots are $x=-7$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-7),$ $(-7,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+7)(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+7)(x-5)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+7)(x-5)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-7)$ and $(5,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-7$ and $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{35}{2}\\ge \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x\n$$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [5,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186253262 :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{det}(L)$, if\n    $L=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate $\\text{det}(L)$, if\n    $$\n\\displaystyle L=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253263 :question/problem "If $g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ for $x>0,$ determine ${g}^{′}(2).$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ for $x>0,$ determine ${g}^{′}(2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186253264 :question/problem "For $x>0,$ find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "For $x>0,$ find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)={\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253265 :question/problem "Determine ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{3x-5}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.$" "Determine ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)={\\int }_{3x-5}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253266 :question/problem "If\n    $M=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & w \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\text{det}(M)=2$, what is the value of $w?$" "If\n    $$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & w \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(M)=2$, what is the value of $w?$"] [:db/cas 17592186253267 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{x}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t.$" "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ if $$\n\\dis