ros of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186253391 :question/worked-solution "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =4n\\pi +\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =4n\\pi +\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186253393 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a433e9e9f2018c1f964f13b0ef248f72253f99f071f44e029c928598e9d04a32.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/a4/a433e9e9f2018c1f964f13b0ef248f72253f99f071f44e029c928598e9d04a32.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253394 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}=\\frac{8}{{V}^{2}-16}.$" "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}=\\frac{8}{{V}^{2}-16}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253395 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}=\\frac{3}{{m}^{2}+m-12}.$" "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}=\\frac{3}{{m}^{2}+m-12}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253396 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{2(s+4)}=\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+3s-4}.$" "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2(s+4)}=\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+3s-4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253400 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function  $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function  $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186253400 :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\pi x}{3} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\pi x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}+3n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}+3n,$ where $n$ is an integer." "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\pi x}{3} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\pi x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}+3n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}+3n,$ where $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186253401 :question/problem "![](images/f7/f725b571954587b7b88426a254d7a6fc05f09b63ab2cc94b81303a510e8c1798.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/f7/f725b571954587b7b88426a254d7a6fc05f09b63ab2cc94b81303a510e8c1798.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253403 :question/problem "What is the domain of the function  $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." "What is the domain of the function  $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186253403 :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =2\\pi +4n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne 2\\pi +4n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =2\\pi +4n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne 2\\pi +4n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186253405 :question/problem "![](images/45/45e506d3b6d3a2ca1a9b16a061f5c081e10b8c578796d7d4a1dfab6e6f793520.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/45/45e506d3b6d3a2ca1a9b16a061f5c081e10b8c578796d7d4a1dfab6e6f793520.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253409 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253409 :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=4\\pi .$" "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=4\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253410 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y=-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253410 :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{csc}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}=12\\pi .$" "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{csc}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}=12\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253414 :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}-0=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\right)}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{5}{4}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}-0=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\right)}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{5}{4}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B+D=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253416 :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3$ at its local minimum points?" "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3\n$$ at its local minimum points?"] [:db/cas 17592186253426 :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+4C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/33/336c38a44a3a40fcaf4a226f61a9f7af2cc731674c1ccc6679b80d846d4ea657.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)$ by $0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ units to the left. Therefore\n\n$$\n4C=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+4C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/33/336c38a44a3a40fcaf4a226f61a9f7af2cc731674c1ccc6679b80d846d4ea657.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)$ by $$\n\\displaystyle 0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$ units to the left. Therefore\n\n$$\n4C=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186253439 :question/problem "![](images/03/03a826b548db2731a6854327d6e11dcc5147d5c21db93f0b17b6b131e5163efc.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/03/03a826b548db2731a6854327d6e11dcc5147d5c21db93f0b17b6b131e5163efc.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253456 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{{d}^{2}-3d}=\\frac{2}{d-3}.$" "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{{d}^{2}-3d}=\\frac{2}{d-3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253457 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{{v}^{2}+4v}=\\frac{3}{v}.$" "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{{v}^{2}+4v}=\\frac{3}{v}.\n$$"]]
